-- Q8 [Standard Reduction Potentials · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g33)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt33$By international convention, all standard reduction potentials are reported relative to a single reference half-cell, the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE), which corresponds to the half-reaction \(2\text{H}^{+}(aq) + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{H}_2(g)\) under standard conditions. What standard reduction potential, \(E^{\circ}\), is assigned to the SHE?$qt33$,
    $op33$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(0.00\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+1.00\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(-0.42\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+0.34\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op33$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex33$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the standard hydrogen electrode is defined as the universal reference half-cell, and by convention it is assigned a standard reduction potential of exactly \(0.00\ \text{V}\) for \(2\text{H}^{+}(aq) + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{H}_2(g)\) at standard conditions (\(1\ \text{M}\ \text{H}^{+}\), \(1\ \text{atm}\ \text{H}_2\), \(298\ \text{K}\)). Because absolute single-electrode potentials cannot be measured in isolation, every other half-cell potential is reported as a value measured against this fixed \(0.00\ \text{V}\) baseline. (Choice B) \(+1.00\ \text{V}\) is not the defined reference value; the SHE is fixed at zero, not at an arbitrary positive number, so this misstates the convention. (Choice C) \(-0.42\ \text{V}\) is a tabulated reduction potential associated with the related hydrogen half-reaction \(2\text{H}^{+} + 2e^{-} \rightarrow \text{H}_2\) at biological conditions (\(\text{pH}\ 7\)), not the standard \(0.00\ \text{V}\) reference at \(1\ \text{M}\ \text{H}^{+}\). (Choice D) \(+0.34\ \text{V}\) is the standard reduction potential of the \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\) half-cell, a value that is itself defined by measurement against the SHE, not the potential of the SHE. Skill 1$ex33$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc33$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc33$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
