-- Q9 [Standard Reduction Potentials · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g34)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt34$A chemist consults a table of standard reduction potentials at 298 K and records the following values: \(\text{Au}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Au}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +1.50\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Sn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Sn}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.14\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Cr}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Cr}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.74\ \text{V}\). The chemist ranks the three solid metals from strongest reducing agent to weakest. Which ranking is correct?$qt34$,
    $op34$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Au}(s) > \\text{Sn}(s) > \\text{Cr}(s)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Cr}(s) > \\text{Sn}(s) > \\text{Au}(s)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Sn}(s) > \\text{Cr}(s) > \\text{Au}(s)\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Cr}(s) > \\text{Au}(s) > \\text{Sn}(s)\\)"}]$op34$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex34$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because a metal's reducing strength tracks how readily it is oxidized, and a species is most easily oxidized when its reduction couple has the MOST NEGATIVE standard reduction potential. Ordering the three couples by \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) gives \(-0.74\ \text{V}\) (\(\text{Cr}^{3+}/\text{Cr}\)) \(< -0.14\ \text{V}\) (\(\text{Sn}^{2+}/\text{Sn}\)) \(< +1.50\ \text{V}\) (\(\text{Au}^{3+}/\text{Au}\)). The most negative potential belongs to chromium, so \(\text{Cr}(s)\) gives up electrons most readily and is the strongest reducing agent; tin is intermediate; and \(\text{Au}(s)\), whose couple has the most positive potential, is the weakest reducing agent. Reducing strength therefore decreases in the order \(\text{Cr}(s) > \text{Sn}(s) > \text{Au}(s)\). (Choice A) \(\text{Au}(s) > \text{Sn}(s) > \text{Cr}(s)\) ranks the metals by INCREASING tendency to be oxidized in reverse, treating the most positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) as the strongest reductant; this inverts the relationship, since the most positive couple actually houses the strongest oxidizing agent (\(\text{Au}^{3+}\)) and the weakest reductant (\(\text{Au}(s)\)). (Choice C) \(\text{Sn}(s) > \text{Cr}(s) > \text{Au}(s)\) correctly places \(\text{Au}(s)\) last but swaps the top two, because \(-0.14\ \text{V}\) is less negative than \(-0.74\ \text{V}\), making \(\text{Sn}(s)\) a weaker reductant than \(\text{Cr}(s)\), not a stronger one. (Choice D) \(\text{Cr}(s) > \text{Au}(s) > \text{Sn}(s)\) correctly identifies \(\text{Cr}(s)\) as strongest but misorders the remaining two, ranking \(\text{Au}(s)\) (\(+1.50\ \text{V}\), the weakest reductant) above \(\text{Sn}(s)\) (\(-0.14\ \text{V}\)). Skill 1$ex34$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc34$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc34$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
