-- Q10 [Standard Reduction Potentials · easy · Skill 1 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g35)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt35$A chemist consults a table of standard reduction potentials and finds the following values at 298 K: \(\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Mg}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -2.37\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.76\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.25\ \text{V}\). Based on these values, which species is the strongest reducing agent?$qt35$,
    $op35$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Ni}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Zn}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Mg}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Mg}^{2+}\\)"}]$op35$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex35$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the strongest reducing agent is the species that is most readily oxidized, i.e., the one that gives up electrons most easily. A reducing agent is the reduced form of a redox couple (the neutral metal), and its tendency to be oxidized is the reverse of the tabulated reduction. The more negative the standard reduction potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\), the less favorable the reduction and therefore the more favorable the corresponding oxidation, making that reduced-form metal a stronger reducing agent. Of the three couples, \(\text{Mg}^{2+}/\text{Mg}\) has the most negative reduction potential (\(-2.37\ \text{V}\), versus \(-0.76\ \text{V}\) for \(\text{Zn}\) and \(-0.25\ \text{V}\) for \(\text{Ni}\)), so magnesium metal is the most easily oxidized and is the strongest reducing agent. (Choice A) Nickel has the least negative reduction potential (\(-0.25\ \text{V}\)) of the three, meaning its cation is the most readily reduced; nickel metal is therefore the weakest, not the strongest, reducing agent among these. (Choice B) Zinc metal has an intermediate reduction potential (\(-0.76\ \text{V}\)), so it is a stronger reducing agent than nickel but a weaker one than magnesium; it is not the strongest. (Choice D) \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) is the oxidized form of the magnesium couple, so it can only act as an oxidizing agent (by gaining electrons), not as a reducing agent. Skill 1$ex35$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc35$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc35$, 'general-chemistry', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
