-- Q11 [Standard Reduction Potentials · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g36)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt36$For the aluminum couple, the standard reduction potential is given as \(\text{Al}^{3+}(aq) + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}(s)\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -1.66\ \text{V}\). A student needs the standard potential for aluminum metal being oxidized, \(\text{Al}(s) \rightarrow \text{Al}^{3+}(aq) + 3e^-\). What is the standard oxidation potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{ox}}\), for this half-reaction?$qt36$,
    $op36$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-4.98\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(-1.66\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+4.98\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+1.66\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op36$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex36$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because reversing the direction of a half-reaction reverses the sign of its standard potential while leaving its magnitude unchanged, and because standard potential is an intensive property it is never multiplied by the number of electrons transferred. The given reduction half-reaction is \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}\) with \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -1.66\ \text{V}\). The requested half-reaction, \(\text{Al} \rightarrow \text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^-\), is exactly this reaction written in reverse, so its potential is the negative of the reduction value: \(E^{\circ}_{\text{ox}} = -E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -(-1.66\ \text{V}) = +1.66\ \text{V}\). The three electrons describe the stoichiometric scale of the reaction but do not enter the potential, because \(E^{\circ}\) is an intensive (per-coulomb) quantity rather than an extensive one. (Choice A) \(-4.98\ \text{V}\) multiplies the magnitude by the three electrons while also failing to flip the sign, combining the scaling error with the omitted sign reversal. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must recognize that reversing a half-reaction inverts the sign of its standard potential and that the potential, being intensive, is independent of the electron count. (Choice B) \(-1.66\ \text{V}\) simply restates the tabulated reduction potential without flipping its sign; it ignores that converting a reduction into an oxidation reverses the reaction and therefore reverses the sign of the potential. (Choice C) \(+4.98\ \text{V}\) flips the sign correctly but then multiplies the magnitude by the three transferred electrons (\(3 \times 1.66\ \text{V}\)), wrongly treating \(E^{\circ}\) as an extensive quantity that scales with the number of electrons. Skill 2$ex36$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc36$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc36$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
