UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'scale_unit_error', $a36C$Flips the sign correctly but multiplies the magnitude by the 3 electrons, treating intensive E as extensive.$a36C$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Standard Reduction Potentials · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g37)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt37$A spontaneous galvanic cell is assembled from two half-cells: an inert Pt electrode in a solution containing both \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) and \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\), and a Pb electrode in \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) solution. The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.77\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Pb}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb}\), \(E^{\circ} = -0.13\ \text{V}\). Which process occurs at the cathode of this cell?$qt37$,
    $op37$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) is reduced to \\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}\\) is reduced to \\(\\text{Pb}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}\\)"}]$op37$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex37$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. In any galvanic (spontaneous) cell, reduction occurs at the cathode, and the couple that actually gets reduced is the one with the more positive standard reduction potential, because that half-reaction has the greater tendency to gain electrons. Comparing the two couples, \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) at \(E^{\circ} = +0.77\ \text{V}\) is far more positive than \(\text{Pb}^{2+}/\text{Pb}\) at \(E^{\circ} = -0.13\ \text{V}\). The answer to this question is A because the more-positive \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) couple is therefore the cathode, where \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) is reduced to \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\); the Pb electrode becomes the anode, where Pb is oxidized to \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\). This assignment also gives the correct, positive cell potential: \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (+0.77) - (-0.13) = +0.90\ \text{V} > 0\), confirming the spontaneous (galvanic) direction. (Choice B) Reducing \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) to Pb is a reduction, but it is assigned to the wrong electrode: the Pb couple has the more negative \(E^{\circ}\), so it is oxidized at the anode, not reduced at the cathode. (Choice C) Oxidizing \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) to \(\text{Fe}^{3+}\) is an oxidation; oxidation occurs at the anode, not the cathode, and here the iron couple is actually the species being reduced. (Choice D) Oxidizing Pb to \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) is the correct anode half-reaction, but the cathode is defined as the reduction electrode, so this process does not occur at the cathode. Skill 2$ex37$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc37$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc37$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
