-- Q13 [Standard Reduction Potentials · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g38)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt38$A nickel strip in \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) solution and a lead strip in \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) solution are joined into a galvanic cell. Their standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni},\ E^{\circ} = -0.25\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Pb}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Pb},\ E^{\circ} = -0.13\ \text{V}\). At the negative electrode of this operating cell, which species is consumed as the cell delivers current?$qt38$,
    $op38$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}(s)\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Ni}(s)\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Ni}^{2+}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Pb}^{2+}\\)"}]$op38$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex38$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the negative electrode of a galvanic cell is the anode, where oxidation occurs, and the species oxidized at the anode is the solid metal of the couple with the more negative standard reduction potential, since a more negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) means that couple gives up electrons more readily. The nickel couple at \(-0.25\ \text{V}\) is more negative than the lead couple at \(-0.13\ \text{V}\), so the nickel half-cell is the anode and \(\text{Ni}(s)\) is consumed via \(\text{Ni}(s) \rightarrow \text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^-\). This assignment is confirmed by the cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (-0.13) - (-0.25) = +0.12\ \text{V}\), which is positive and therefore consistent with spontaneous galvanic operation in which nickel is the anode. (Choice A) \(\text{Pb}(s)\) belongs to the couple with the more positive (less negative) reduction potential, so lead is the cathode metal; \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) is reduced to \(\text{Pb}(s)\) there and lead metal is deposited rather than consumed, and the lead electrode is the positive terminal, not the negative one. (Choice C) \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) is the product of nickel oxidation that accumulates in the anode solution, not a species consumed at the negative electrode; consuming \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) by reduction would require the nickel half-cell to be the cathode, which contradicts its more negative potential. (Choice D) \(\text{Pb}^{2+}\) is consumed by reduction at the cathode (the positive electrode), where it gains electrons to form \(\text{Pb}(s)\), so it acts as the oxidizing agent at the wrong electrode rather than as the species consumed at the negative electrode. Skill 2$ex38$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc38$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc38$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
