-- Q14 [Standard Reduction Potentials · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g39)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt39$The following standard reduction potentials are measured at 298 K: \(\text{Cl}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{Cl}^-,\ E^{\circ} = +1.36\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu},\ E^{\circ} = +0.34\ \text{V}\); and \(\text{Fe}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe},\ E^{\circ} = -0.44\ \text{V}\). Based on these values alone, which ranking correctly orders the oxidized species from the strongest oxidizing agent to the weakest?$qt39$,
    $op39$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Fe}^{2+} > \\text{Cu}^{2+} > \\text{Cl}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2 > \\text{Fe}^{2+} > \\text{Cu}^{2+}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Cl}_2 > \\text{Cu}^{2+} > \\text{Fe}^{2+}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Cu}^{2+} > \\text{Cl}_2 > \\text{Fe}^{2+}\\)"}]$op39$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex39$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the standard reduction potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) directly measures how readily the oxidized form (the species written on the left of each half-reaction) accepts electrons and is reduced. The species that is reduced in a redox process is, by definition, the oxidizing agent. Therefore, the more positive a couple's \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\), the more strongly its oxidized form pulls in electrons, and the stronger an oxidizing agent that oxidized form is. Ranking the three values in order of decreasing (most positive to most negative) potential gives \(+1.36\ \text{V}\) for \(\text{Cl}_2/\text{Cl}^-\), then \(+0.34\ \text{V}\) for \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\), then \(-0.44\ \text{V}\) for \(\text{Fe}^{2+}/\text{Fe}\). The oxidized species are \(\text{Cl}_2\), \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\), and \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\), so the order from strongest to weakest oxidizing agent is \(\text{Cl}_2 > \text{Cu}^{2+} > \text{Fe}^{2+}\). Note that the sign and the full magnitude both matter: \(-0.44\ \text{V}\) is the lowest value because it is the most negative, marking \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) as the weakest oxidizing agent (and, equivalently, marking \(\text{Fe}\) metal as the strongest reducing agent of the three). (Choice A) reverses the relationship by treating the most negative potential as the strongest oxidizing agent; a more negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) indicates a species less inclined to be reduced, so \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) is actually the weakest, not the strongest, oxidizing agent. (Choice B) correctly places \(\text{Cl}_2\) first but then ranks the remaining two by the magnitude of their potentials while ignoring sign, treating \(|-0.44|\) as larger than \(|+0.34|\); because a negative potential lies below a positive one on the scale, \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) (\(+0.34\ \text{V}\)) is the stronger oxidizing agent and must precede \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\) (\(-0.44\ \text{V}\)). (Choice D) misapplies metal-activity intuition by ranking the comparatively unreactive copper ion ahead of the nonmetal \(\text{Cl}_2\); oxidizing strength is set by \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\), and \(\text{Cl}_2\) at \(+1.36\ \text{V}\) far exceeds \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) at \(+0.34\ \text{V}\), so \(\text{Cl}_2\) must come first. Skill 2$ex39$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc39$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc39$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
