-- Q15 [Standard Reduction Potentials · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g40)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt40$A chemist has three redox couples available, with standard reduction potentials at 298 K: \(\text{Li}^{+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Li}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = -3.04\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Fe}^{3+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}^{2+}\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.77\ \text{V}\); and \(\text{F}_2(g) + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{F}^-\), \(E^{\circ} = +2.87\ \text{V}\). She wants to assemble a single galvanic cell that operates spontaneously as written. Each candidate below specifies one pairing of two couples, identifies which couple's reduction occurs at the cathode, and names the species that is oxidized at the anode. Which candidate describes a cell that is spontaneous under standard conditions with both electrodes correctly assigned?$qt40$,
    $op40$[{"label":"A","text":"Pair \\(\\text{Li}^{+}/\\text{Li}\\) with \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}/\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\); \\(\\text{Li}^{+}\\) is reduced at the cathode and \\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\) is oxidized at the anode."},{"label":"B","text":"Pair \\(\\text{F}_2/\\text{F}^-\\) with \\(\\text{Li}^{+}/\\text{Li}\\); \\(\\text{Li}^{+}\\) is reduced at the cathode and \\(\\text{F}^-\\) is oxidized at the anode."},{"label":"C","text":"Pair \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}/\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\) with \\(\\text{F}_2/\\text{F}^-\\); \\(\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\) is reduced at the cathode and \\(\\text{F}^-\\) is oxidized at the anode."},{"label":"D","text":"Pair \\(\\text{F}_2/\\text{F}^-\\) with \\(\\text{Li}^{+}/\\text{Li}\\); \\(\\text{F}_2\\) is reduced at the cathode and \\(\\text{Li}\\) is oxidized at the anode."}]$op40$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex40$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because a galvanic cell is spontaneous only when \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} > 0\), which requires the couple with the more positive standard reduction potential to serve as the cathode (reduction) and the couple with the more negative reduction potential to serve as the anode (oxidation). Ranking the three couples by \(E^{\circ}\) gives \(\text{F}_2/\text{F}^-\) (\(+2.87\ \text{V}\)) as the strongest oxidizing agent and \(\text{Li}^{+}/\text{Li}\) (\(-3.04\ \text{V}\)) as the weakest oxidizing agent, meaning \(\text{Li}\) metal is the strongest reducing agent. Pairing the highest and lowest couples and assigning \(\text{F}_2\) reduction to the cathode while \(\text{Li}\) is oxidized at the anode gives \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (+2.87) - (-3.04) = +5.91\ \text{V}\). Because \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\), so the cell is spontaneous, and the assignment (\(\text{F}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\,\text{F}^-\) at the cathode; \(\text{Li} \rightarrow \text{Li}^{+} + e^-\) at the anode) is correct, with electrons flowing from the \(\text{Li}\) anode to the \(\text{F}_2\) cathode. (Choice A) correctly recognizes \(\text{Li}^{+}/\text{Li}\) and \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) as a real pairing but assigns the most negative couple, \(\text{Li}^{+}/\text{Li}\), as the cathode; this inverts the required ranking and gives \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (-3.04) - (+0.77) = -3.81\ \text{V} < 0\), the nonspontaneous direction. (Choice B) reverses the electrode roles of the correct pairing, making \(\text{Li}^{+}/\text{Li}\) the cathode and \(\text{F}_2/\text{F}^-\) the anode; this gives \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (-3.04) - (+2.87) = -5.91\ \text{V} < 0\), which is the nonspontaneous (electrolytic) direction, so the cell does not operate spontaneously as written. (Choice C) keeps \(\text{F}_2/\text{F}^-\) but assigns it as the anode (oxidizing \(\text{F}^-\)) while making the weaker oxidant \(\text{Fe}^{3+}/\text{Fe}^{2+}\) the cathode; since the cathode must be the more positive couple, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (+0.77) - (+2.87) = -2.10\ \text{V} < 0\), so it is nonspontaneous. Skill 2$ex40$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc40$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc40$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a40A$Picks a valid pairing but assigns the most negative couple (Li+/Li) as the cathode instead of the anode, inverting the reduction-potential ranking; E_cell = -3.81 V.$a40A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a40B$Reverses the electrode roles of the correct top-vs-bottom pairing, making the most negative couple the cathode and the most positive the anode, yielding E_cell = -5.91 V.$a40B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a40C$Treats the strongest oxidizing agent (F2/F-) as the anode and a weaker couple (Fe3+/Fe2+) as the cathode, ignoring that the more positive couple must be reduced; E_cell = -2.10 V.$a40C$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Cell Potential & EMF · easy · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g41)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt41$A galvanic cell is constructed under standard conditions (298 K, 1 M ion concentrations) by connecting a zinc electrode immersed in \(\text{Zn}^{2+}\) solution to a nickel electrode immersed in \(\text{Ni}^{2+}\) solution with a salt bridge. The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.76\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Ni}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Ni}\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.25\ \text{V}\). For the spontaneous operation of this cell, what is the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\)?$qt41$,
    $op41$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+0.51\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+1.01\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(-0.51\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+0.25\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op41$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex41$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because for a spontaneous galvanic cell the electrode with the more positive standard reduction potential serves as the cathode (reduction) and the more negative one serves as the anode (oxidation). Here \(\text{Ni}^{2+}/\text{Ni}\) (\(-0.25\ \text{V}\)) is more positive than \(\text{Zn}^{2+}/\text{Zn}\) (\(-0.76\ \text{V}\)), so Ni is the cathode and Zn is the anode. Applying \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (-0.25\ \text{V}) - (-0.76\ \text{V}) = +0.51\ \text{V}\). The positive result confirms the cell is spontaneous as assigned. (Choice B) \(+1.01\ \text{V}\) results from adding the magnitudes of the two potentials (\(0.76 + 0.25\)) instead of subtracting the signed reduction potentials; the defining equation requires a difference of signed values, not a sum. (Choice C) \(-0.51\ \text{V}\) reverses the order of subtraction, computing \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} = (-0.76) - (-0.25)\); a spontaneous galvanic cell must yield a positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), so this sign is incorrect. (Choice D) \(+0.25\ \text{V}\) uses only the magnitude of the cathode (Ni) reduction potential and omits the anode contribution entirely; both half-cell potentials must enter the calculation. Skill 2$ex41$,
    'easy', '4C', $cc41$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc41$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
