-- Q22 [Standard Reduction Potentials · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g89)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt89$A galvanic cell is to be constructed, and a student must choose which metal in the cell will serve as the stronger reducing agent (the species more readily oxidized). The relevant standard reduction potentials are: \(\text{Sn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Sn}(s),\ E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.14\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Co}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Co}(s),\ E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.28\ \text{V}\). Based on these values, which solid metal is the stronger reducing agent?$qt89$,
    $op89$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Co}(s)\\), because its half-reaction has the more negative standard reduction potential"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Sn}(s)\\), because its half-reaction has the less negative (more positive) standard reduction potential"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Sn}^{2+}(aq)\\), because the more positive \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{red}}\\) identifies the species most readily oxidized"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Co}(s)\\), because the more negative standard reduction potential means the species is most readily reduced"}]$op89$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex89$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the standard reduction potential measures the tendency of a species to be reduced (to gain electrons): the more positive the \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\), the more readily the species is reduced, while the more negative the \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\), the harder it is to reduce and therefore the more readily the corresponding solid metal is oxidized. A reducing agent is itself oxidized, so the stronger reducing agent is the metal that is most easily oxidized, which is the one whose reduction half-reaction has the more negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\). Comparing the two values, \(\text{Co}^{2+}/\text{Co}\) has \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.28\ \text{V}\), which is more negative than \(\text{Sn}^{2+}/\text{Sn}\) at \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.14\ \text{V}\). Because \(-0.28\ \text{V} < -0.14\ \text{V}\), cobalt metal is oxidized more readily than tin metal, making \(\text{Co}(s)\) the stronger reducing agent. (Choice B) This selects \(\text{Sn}(s)\) on the basis that its half-reaction has the less negative potential, but the less negative (more positive) \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) identifies the species more easily reduced, i.e., the stronger oxidizing agent, not the stronger reducing agent. (Choice C) This reaches for a more-positive-\(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) species and also names the oxidized form \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\); a dissolved cation cannot be a reducing agent here because it is already in its higher oxidation state, and the more positive potential corresponds to being most readily reduced, the opposite of what a reducing agent does. (Choice D) This identifies the correct metal, \(\text{Co}(s)\), but pairs it with reversed reasoning: a more negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) means the species is harder to reduce and therefore more readily oxidized, not most readily reduced. Skill 2$ex89$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc89$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc89$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
