SELECT id, 'B', 'misconception', $a89B$picks the less-negative (more positive) E°red as the stronger reducer, confusing it with the stronger oxidizer$a89B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'reversed_relationship', $a89C$selects the more-positive-E°red species and names the already-oxidized cation as the reducing agent$a89C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a89D$correct metal but reversed rationale: claims more negative E°red means most readily reduced$a89D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Standard Reduction Potentials · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g90)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Standard Reduction Potentials',
    $qt90$An electrochemistry student sets up a single cell containing a copper electrode in \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) solution and an inert electrode in a solution containing both \(\text{Br}^-\) and \(\text{Br}_2\). The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu},\ E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +0.34\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Br}_2 + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\text{Br}^-,\ E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +1.07\ \text{V}\). Instead of letting the cell run on its own, the student connects an external power source and adjusts it so that copper metal is steadily deposited onto the copper electrode. Under this driven operation, which species is oxidized, and what does the requirement reveal about the cell?$qt90$,
    $op90$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(\\text{Cu}\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) at the anode; the less positive couple (\\(+0.34\\ \\text{V}\\)) always supplies the oxidation, and the cell runs spontaneously with \\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +0.73\\ \\text{V}\\)."},{"label":"B","text":"\\(\\text{Br}^-\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Br}_2\\) at the anode; because the more positive couple (\\(+1.07\\ \\text{V}\\)) is forced to undergo oxidation against the spontaneous direction, the process is non-spontaneous and requires an applied voltage of at least \\(0.73\\ \\text{V}\\)."},{"label":"C","text":"\\(\\text{Br}_2\\) is reduced to \\(\\text{Br}^-\\) at the anode; the more positive couple (\\(+1.07\\ \\text{V}\\)) is reduced, so the driven cell is spontaneous and needs no minimum applied voltage."},{"label":"D","text":"\\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) is oxidized to \\(\\text{Cu}\\) at the anode; depositing copper is an oxidation that the external source merely speeds up, and the process is spontaneous with \\(E_{\\text{cell}} = +1.07\\ \\text{V}\\)."}]$op90$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex90$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because the driving constraint inverts the ordinary galvanic rule. Left alone, the spontaneous (galvanic) cell would put the couple with the more positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) at the cathode: \(\text{Br}_2/\text{Br}^-\ (+1.07\ \text{V})\) would be reduced and \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\ (+0.34\ \text{V})\) would be oxidized, giving \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = 1.07 - 0.34 = +0.73\ \text{V} > 0\). However, the external power source is adjusted to deposit copper, i.e., to force \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\) (reduction) at the cathode. By elimination, oxidation must occur at the other electrode, so \(2\text{Br}^- \rightarrow \text{Br}_2 + 2e^-\) is driven at the anode — even though \(\text{Br}_2/\text{Br}^-\) is the more positive (more easily reduced) couple. For this forced configuration, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = E^{\circ}(\text{Cu}) - E^{\circ}(\text{Br}) = 0.34 - 1.07 = -0.73\ \text{V} < 0\), so \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\): the operation is non-spontaneous (electrolytic) and requires an external voltage of at least \(0.73\ \text{V}\) to drive it. (Choice A) This blindly applies the galvanic rule (less positive couple is oxidized) and reports the spontaneous \(+0.73\ \text{V}\) cell, but the stated constraint deposits copper, forcing \(\text{Cu}^{2+}\) to be reduced — not \(\text{Cu}\) oxidized — so this describes the cell running backward from what the power source enforces. (Choice C) This calls a reduction (\(\text{Br}_2 \rightarrow \text{Br}^-\)) an anode process, which is a contradiction because oxidation, by definition, occurs at the anode; it also wrongly concludes the driven cell is spontaneous. (Choice D) Depositing copper, \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\), is a reduction (gain of electrons) occurring at the cathode, not an oxidation at the anode, and \(+1.07\ \text{V}\) is merely a tabulated reduction potential, not the cell potential of this configuration. Skill 2$ex90$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc90$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc90$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a90A$applies the galvanic less-positive-couple-oxidizes rule despite the forcing constraint$a90A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a90C$labels a reduction as an anode (oxidation) process and calls the driven cell spontaneous$a90C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a90D$misnames copper deposition (a reduction at the cathode) as oxidation at the anode$a90D$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Cell Potential & EMF · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g91)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt91$A galvanic cell is assembled by connecting a tin electrode immersed in 1 M \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) to a silver electrode immersed in 1 M \(\text{Ag}^{+}\), joined by a wire and a salt bridge at 298 K. The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Sn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Sn}(s)\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.14\ \text{V}\), and \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag}(s)\), \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +0.80\ \text{V}\). What is the standard cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), of this cell?$qt91$,
    $op91$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+1.74\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+0.66\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+0.94\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-0.94\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op91$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex91$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. In a galvanic cell, the half-reaction with the more positive standard reduction potential proceeds as reduction at the cathode, while the other is forced to run in reverse as oxidation at the anode. Here \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +0.80\ \text{V}\)) is more positive than \(\text{Sn}^{2+}\) (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.14\ \text{V}\)), so silver is the cathode and tin is the anode. The answer to this question is C because \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red, cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red, anode}} = (+0.80\ \text{V}) - (-0.14\ \text{V}) = +0.94\ \text{V}\). Crucially, standard reduction potential is an intensive property: even though balancing the overall reaction requires two \(\text{Ag}^{+}\) ions per \(\text{Sn}\) atom (\(\text{Sn} + 2\text{Ag}^{+} \rightarrow \text{Sn}^{2+} + 2\text{Ag}\)), the silver potential is NOT multiplied by 2, because \(E^{\circ}\) does not scale with the amount of substance. (Choice A) \(+1.74\ \text{V}\) results from incorrectly multiplying the silver potential by its stoichiometric coefficient, \((2 \times 0.80) - (-0.14) = +1.74\ \text{V}\); this violates the fact that \(E^{\circ}\) is intensive and never scaled by coefficients. (Choice B) \(+0.66\ \text{V}\) comes from \(0.80 - 0.14\), an arithmetic slip in which the negative sign of the anode's reduction potential is dropped so that its magnitude is subtracted rather than the negative value. (Choice D) \(-0.94\ \text{V}\) reverses the assignment of cathode and anode, computing \((-0.14) - (+0.80)\); the more positive couple must be the cathode, and a spontaneous galvanic cell yields a positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\). Skill 2$ex91$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc91$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc91$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a91A$multiplied an intensive E-degree by the stoichiometric coefficient$a91A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a91B$dropped the negative sign of the anode potential and subtracted its magnitude$a91B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a91D$swapped cathode and anode in the E-degree-cell subtraction$a91D$ FROM q;

-- Q25 [Cell Potential & EMF · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g92)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt92$A chemist has three half-cells available, each consisting of a metal electrode immersed in a 1 M solution of its own ion at 298 K. The standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}\), \(E^{\circ} = -0.76\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.34\ \text{V}\); and \(\text{Ag}^{+} + e^- \rightarrow \text{Ag}\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.80\ \text{V}\). The chemist wants to connect two of these half-cells to build the galvanic cell with the greatest standard EMF. Which combination should be chosen, and what is the resulting \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\)?$qt92$,
    $op92$[{"label":"A","text":"Ag electrode as the anode and Zn electrode as the cathode, giving \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +1.56\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"Zn electrode as the anode and Cu electrode as the cathode, giving \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +1.10\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cu electrode as the anode and Ag electrode as the cathode, giving \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +0.46\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"Zn electrode as the anode and Ag electrode as the cathode, giving \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\text{cell}} = +1.56\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op92$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex92$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the standard EMF of a galvanic cell is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}}\), and this is maximized by pairing the couple with the most positive reduction potential (which is reduced at the cathode) with the couple having the most negative reduction potential (which is oxidized at the anode). Among the three couples, \(\text{Ag}^{+}/\text{Ag}\) at \(+0.80\ \text{V}\) is the strongest oxidizing agent (most positive, so it serves as the cathode) and \(\text{Zn}^{2+}/\text{Zn}\) at \(-0.76\ \text{V}\) is the strongest reducing agent (most negative, so Zn metal is oxidized at the anode). This gives \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (+0.80\ \text{V}) - (-0.76\ \text{V}) = +1.56\ \text{V}\). Comparing all three possible cells: Zn/Ag = \(+0.80 - (-0.76) = +1.56\ \text{V}\); Zn/Cu = \(+0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10\ \text{V}\); Cu/Ag = \(+0.80 - (+0.34) = +0.46\ \text{V}\). The Zn anode / Ag cathode pairing produces the largest value, \(+1.56\ \text{V}\), and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\) confirms the reaction is spontaneous as written, consistent with a galvanic cell. (Choice A) This reverses the electrode roles: assigning Ag as the anode and Zn as the cathode would force the spontaneous process to run backward, giving \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = -0.76 - (+0.80) = -1.56\ \text{V}\) (non-spontaneous, an electrolytic configuration), so reporting it as \(+1.56\ \text{V}\) for a galvanic cell is incorrect. (Choice B) The Zn anode / Cu cathode pairing is a valid galvanic cell, but \(+1.10\ \text{V}\) is smaller than the Zn/Ag value because \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\) is a weaker oxidizing agent than \(\text{Ag}^{+}/\text{Ag}\); this choice selects a correctly computed but non-maximal cell. (Choice C) The Cu anode / Ag cathode pairing yields only \(+0.46\ \text{V}\) because it fails to use Zn, the strongest reducing agent; omitting the most negative couple gives the smallest of the three EMFs. Skill 2$ex92$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc92$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc92$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
