UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a92B$valid but non-maximal cell pairing (Zn/Cu instead of Zn/Ag)$a92B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a92C$omits the strongest reducing agent (Zn), pairing the two higher couples$a92C$ FROM q;

-- Q26 [Cell Potential & EMF · medium · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans A] (g93)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt93$A galvanic cell is constructed using a magnesium electrode immersed in 1 M \(\text{Mg}^{2+}\) and an iron electrode immersed in 1 M \(\text{Fe}^{2+}\), both at 298 K under standard conditions. The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Mg}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Mg}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = -2.37\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Fe}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Fe}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = -0.44\ \text{V}\). If the cell operates spontaneously, what is the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\)?$qt93$,
    $op93$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+1.93\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+2.81\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(-1.93\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-2.81\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op93$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex93$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because in a spontaneous galvanic cell the couple with the higher (less negative) standard reduction potential serves as the cathode, where reduction occurs. Here \(\text{Fe}^{2+}/\text{Fe}\) (\(E^{\circ} = -0.44\ \text{V}\)) is less negative than \(\text{Mg}^{2+}/\text{Mg}\) (\(E^{\circ} = -2.37\ \text{V}\)), so iron is the cathode and magnesium is the anode (oxidation). Applying \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}}\) gives \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (-0.44\ \text{V}) - (-2.37\ \text{V}) = -0.44\ \text{V} + 2.37\ \text{V} = +1.93\ \text{V}\). The positive value confirms a spontaneous galvanic process (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)), consistent with the problem's stipulation. (Choice B) \(+2.81\ \text{V}\) results from adding the magnitudes of the two potentials (\(2.37 + 0.44\)) rather than subtracting the cathode and anode reduction potentials with their actual signs; the subtraction formula already accounts for the sign of the anode, so flipping and adding double-counts it. (Choice C) \(-1.93\ \text{V}\) has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign, which would arise from reversing the assignment of cathode and anode (treating Mg as the cathode); that assignment would make the cell nonspontaneous and contradicts the requirement of spontaneous operation. (Choice D) \(-2.81\ \text{V}\) combines both errors, summing the magnitudes and then assigning the wrong sign, describing neither the correct nor a physically consistent galvanic configuration. Skill 2$ex93$,
    'medium', '4C', $cc93$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc93$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 110
  ) RETURNING id
)
