-- Q27 [Cell Potential & EMF · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans B] (g94)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt94$A laboratory keeps four redox couples available, each characterized by its standard reduction potential at 298 K: \(\text{F}_2(g) + 2e^- \rightarrow 2\text{F}^-\), \(E^{\circ} = +2.87\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = +0.34\ \text{V}\); \(\text{Zn}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Zn}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = -0.76\ \text{V}\); and \(\text{Li}^+ + e^- \rightarrow \text{Li}(s)\), \(E^{\circ} = -3.04\ \text{V}\). A student is asked to combine exactly two of these couples into a single galvanic cell that delivers the largest possible spontaneous standard cell potential. Using the convention \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}}\), what is the value of \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) for the optimal cell?$qt94$,
    $op94$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-5.91\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+5.91\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+3.63\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(-0.17\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op94$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex94$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is B because maximizing a spontaneous \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) requires pairing the couple with the highest reduction potential (which becomes the cathode) with the couple with the lowest reduction potential (which becomes the anode), and then subtracting. The most positive \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) is \(\text{F}_2/\text{F}^-\) at \(+2.87\ \text{V}\); fluorine is the strongest oxidant, so it is reduced at the cathode. The most negative \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}}\) is \(\text{Li}^+/\text{Li}\) at \(-3.04\ \text{V}\); the more negative the reduction potential, the more readily the species is oxidized, so lithium metal is the strongest reductant and is oxidized at the anode. Applying the convention exactly once, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (+2.87\ \text{V}) - (-3.04\ \text{V}) = +5.91\ \text{V}\). The result is positive, confirming \(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\) and a spontaneous galvanic process, and no other pairing produces a larger gap between the chosen cathode and anode potentials, so \(+5.91\ \text{V}\) is the maximum. (Choice A) \(-5.91\ \text{V}\) reverses the cathode and anode assignments, oxidizing \(\text{F}^-\) and reducing \(\text{Li}^+\), which computes \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = (-3.04) - (+2.87) = -5.91\ \text{V}\); this is the correct magnitude but corresponds to the non-spontaneous (electrolytic) direction, not the largest spontaneous potential requested. (Choice C) \(+3.63\ \text{V}\) keeps \(\text{F}_2\) as the cathode but selects \(\text{Zn}\) rather than \(\text{Li}\) as the anode, \((+2.87) - (-0.76) = +3.63\ \text{V}\); although zinc is a good reductant, its reduction potential is not the most negative, so this pairing does not maximize the cell potential. (Choice D) \(-0.17\ \text{V}\) commits the double-sign error the convention warns against: the student first flips lithium's reduction potential to its oxidation potential \((+3.04\ \text{V})\) and then also subtracts that flipped value, \((+2.87) - (+3.04) = -0.17\ \text{V}\); because the subtraction already accounts for reversing the anode half-reaction, flipping the sign a second time double-counts the reversal and yields an incorrect, near-zero potential. Skill 2$ex94$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc94$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc94$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
