UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a94C$Picks zinc as the strongest reductant instead of lithium, pairing F2 cathode with Zn anode: (+2.87) - (-0.76) = +3.63 V.$a94C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a94D$Double-counts the anode reversal by flipping lithium to its oxidation potential (+3.04 V) and then also subtracting it: (+2.87) - (+3.04) = -0.17 V.$a94D$ FROM q;

-- Q28 [Cell Potential & EMF · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans C] (g95)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt95$A galvanic cell is constructed to drive the balanced reaction \(2\text{Al}(s) + 3\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{Al}^{3+}(aq) + 3\text{Cu}(s)\). The relevant standard reduction potentials are \(\text{Al}^{3+} + 3e^- \rightarrow \text{Al}(s),\ E^{\circ} = -1.66\ \text{V}\) and \(\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{Cu}(s),\ E^{\circ} = +0.34\ \text{V}\). Under standard conditions, what is the standard cell potential \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\) for this reaction as written?$qt95$,
    $op95$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(-2.00\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+4.34\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+2.00\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+1.32\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op95$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex95$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because standard cell potential is an intensive property: it depends only on the identities of the two half-cells, not on the stoichiometric coefficients used to balance the overall equation. To find \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), first identify the electrodes. Copper is reduced (\(\text{Cu}^{2+} \rightarrow \text{Cu}\)), so it is the cathode; aluminum is oxidized (\(\text{Al} \rightarrow \text{Al}^{3+}\)), so it is the anode. Apply \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}}\) using the tabulated reduction potentials directly (do not also flip the anode's sign — the subtraction already accounts for the reversal): \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = (+0.34\ \text{V}) - (-1.66\ \text{V}) = +2.00\ \text{V}\). The positive value confirms the reaction is spontaneous (\(\Delta G^{\circ} = -nFE^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} < 0\)), consistent with a galvanic cell. Crucially, even though balancing the equation required multiplying the Al half-reaction by 2 and the Cu half-reaction by 3 (to transfer 6 electrons), those coefficients are NOT applied to the potentials — \(E^{\circ}\) is in volts (joules per coulomb), an energy-per-charge ratio, and scaling both the energy and the charge leaves the ratio unchanged. (Choice A) \(-2.00\ \text{V}\) reverses the subtraction, computing \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} = -1.66 - 0.34 = -2.00\ \text{V}\); this assigns the wrong electrode as cathode and would incorrectly imply a nonspontaneous (electrolytic) process. (Choice B) \(+4.34\ \text{V}\) results from incorrectly multiplying each half-cell potential by its stoichiometric coefficient: \(3(0.34) + 2(1.66) = 1.02 + 3.32 = 4.34\ \text{V}\). This treats \(E^{\circ}\) as an extensive quantity that scales with coefficients, which is exactly the error to avoid; \(\Delta G^{\circ}\) is extensive, but \(E^{\circ}\) is intensive. (Choice D) \(+1.32\ \text{V}\) comes from taking the magnitudes of the two reduction potentials and subtracting them (\(1.66 - 0.34\)), mishandling the signs rather than performing the proper signed subtraction \((+0.34) - (-1.66)\). Skill 2$ex95$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc95$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc95$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
