-- Q29 [Cell Potential & EMF · hard · Skill 2 · 4C Electrochemistry (GC) · ans D] (g96)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'chem_phys', 'Electrochemistry', 'Cell Potential & EMF',
    $qt96$A metal X forms the half-reaction \(\text{X}^{2+} + 2e^- \rightarrow \text{X}\). When a standard galvanic cell is built from this couple and the \(\text{Zn}^{2+}/\text{Zn}\) couple (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = -0.76\ \text{V}\)), the zinc electrode serves as the anode and the measured standard cell potential is \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = +0.61\ \text{V}\). The same metal X is then used to build a second standard cell paired instead with the \(\text{Cu}^{2+}/\text{Cu}\) couple (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{red}} = +0.34\ \text{V}\)), in which copper is found to be the cathode. What is the standard cell potential, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}}\), of this second (copper-paired) cell?$qt96$,
    $op96$[{"label":"A","text":"\\(+1.10\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"\\(+0.19\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"C","text":"\\(+0.61\\ \\text{V}\\)"},{"label":"D","text":"\\(+0.49\\ \\text{V}\\)"}]$op96$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex96$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Electrochemistry (GC)'. The answer to this question is D because the standard reduction potential of the \(\text{X}^{2+}/\text{X}\) couple must first be backed out of the first cell, then applied to the second pairing. In the first cell, zinc is the anode and X is therefore the cathode, so \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,X}} - (-0.76\ \text{V}) = +0.61\ \text{V}\). Solving, \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,X}} = +0.61\ \text{V} + (-0.76\ \text{V}) = -0.15\ \text{V}\). In the second cell copper is the cathode (so X is the anode), and \(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,cathode}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,anode}} = E^{\circ}_{\text{red,Cu}} - E^{\circ}_{\text{red,X}} = (+0.34\ \text{V}) - (-0.15\ \text{V}) = +0.49\ \text{V}\). The positive value confirms a spontaneous galvanic cell (\(E^{\circ}_{\text{cell}} > 0\), \(\Delta G^{\circ} < 0\)), consistent with copper being the cathode. (Choice A) \(+1.10\ \text{V}\) is the standard potential of the \(\text{Zn}/\text{Cu}\) (Daniell) cell, \(0.34 - (-0.76) = +1.10\ \text{V}\); it pairs copper with zinc rather than with the unknown metal X, using the wrong couple entirely. (Choice B) \(+0.19\ \text{V}\) drops the negative sign on the backed-out potential, treating \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,X}}\) as \(+0.15\ \text{V}\) and computing \(0.34 - 0.15 = +0.19\ \text{V}\); the value obtained in the first step is \(-0.15\ \text{V}\), and reversing its sign before the second subtraction gives the wrong magnitude. (Choice C) \(+0.61\ \text{V}\) simply reuses the measured potential of the first (zinc-paired) cell without re-deriving \(E^{\circ}_{\text{red,X}}\) and applying it to the new copper pairing, so it answers a different cell than the one asked about. Skill 2$ex96$,
    'hard', '4C', $cc96$Electrochemistry (GC)$cc96$, 'general-chemistry', 2, 160
  ) RETURNING id
)
